import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "HTTP: other servers, bodies, cookies",
	description: "HTTP on the Roc Solid server platform: Http.fetch! and its limits, kept-alive connections and proxies, answers read as they arrive, request bodies as bytes, forwarded answers, cookies, and routes for .well-known paths and every method.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "out", label: "Asking another server" },
		{ id: "limits-out", label: "Limits and errors" },
		{ id: "connections", label: "Connections and proxies" },
		{ id: "chunks", label: "Reading an answer as it arrives" },
		{ id: "bytes", label: "Request bodies are bytes" },
		{ id: "forward", label: "Passing on a body from elsewhere" },
		{ id: "cookies", label: "Cookies" },
		{ id: "routes", label: ".well-known, and every method" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · HTTP"
				title="Other servers, bodies as they came, and cookies"
			>
				The server answers HTTP, and asks too: an API's JSON, a sign-in provider's token, a linked page.
				This page covers requests to other servers, request bodies, raw and forwarded answers, and
				cookies.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Every sample here is from <code>server/test/app/Samples.roc</code>, compiled and run by the server
				platform's tests.
			</p>

			<Heading id="out">Asking another server</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_forecast.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_forecast.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Http.fetch!(url, init)"}</code> works like JavaScript's <code>fetch</code>: it sends a
				request and waits for the whole answer, holding no thread meanwhile
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#calls"}>how calls pause</a>). <code>init</code> is an
				<code>Http.Init</code> record. Every field has a default, so <code>{"{}"}</code> is a plain GET:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>method</code>: <code>Get</code>, <code>Post</code>, <code>Put</code>, <code>Patch</code>,
					<code>Delete</code>, <code>Head</code>, <code>Options</code>, or <code>{"Other(\"PROPFIND\")"}</code>
					for any other, sent as written.
				</li>
				<li><code>headers</code>: <code>{"(name, value)"}</code> pairs, sent as given.</li>
				<li>
					<code>query</code>: pairs, percent-encoded and added to the URL's query (after an
					<code>&amp;</code> if it has one).
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>body</code>: <code>Empty</code> (the default), or <code>{"Http.json(value)"}</code>,
					<code>{"Http.form(pairs)"}</code>, <code>{"Http.plain_text(text)"}</code> or
					<code>{"Http.bytes(list)"}</code>. Each sets <code>content-type</code> unless
					<code>headers</code> has one; bytes set none, like <code>fetch</code> with a
					<code>Uint8Array</code>.
				</li>
				<li><code>timeout_ms</code>, <code>max_bytes</code> and <code>redirect</code>: the limits, below.</li>
				<li><code>proxy</code>: <code>FromEnv</code>, <code>Direct</code> or <code>{"Via(url)"}</code>, below.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A record literal works wherever an <code>Http.Init</code> is expected. To keep one and build on it,
				annotate it (as <code>authed</code> above) and update it: <code>{"{ ..authed, method: Delete }"}</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Any status is an answer: <code>Ok</code> holds an <code>HttpResponse</code> with
				<code>res.status</code>, <code>res.ok</code> (true for a 2xx), <code>res.headers</code>,
				<code>{"res.header(name)"}</code> (any case), <code>{"res.text()"}</code>,
				<code>{"res.json()"}</code> (decoded as the type you ask for, or <code>InvalidJson(why)</code>) and
				<code>{"res.bytes()"}</code>. <code>Err</code> means no answer at all.
			</p>
			<p>
				An older API (<code>Http.get</code>, <code>post_form</code>, <code>post_json</code>,
				<code>with_header</code>, <code>{"send!(request, opts)"}</code>, <code>send_request!</code>,
				<code>Http.header</code>, <code>Http.text</code>) is deprecated; each is <code>fetch!</code>
				underneath. The <a href={Paths.ecosystem_storage}>storage package</a> takes <code>Http.send!</code>
				because it names no platform, so it can't name <code>Http.Init</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits-out">Limits and errors</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_preview.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_preview.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				By default a request may take 30 seconds (<code>timeout_ms</code>), its body up to 64 MiB
				(<code>max_bytes</code>), and redirects are followed (<code>redirect: Follow</code>). For a URL a
				stranger gave you, ask for less, as above. <code>0</code> means no limit; <code>Manual</code> returns
				a redirect as the answer. The errors:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>HttpErr(why)</code>: no answer. The address was unreachable, the connection or TLS failed,
					the server broke HTTP, or a header had a line break.
				</li>
				<li><code>TimedOut</code>: no whole answer in time, counted from connecting to the body's last byte.</li>
				<li>
					<code>TooLarge</code>: the body was over the limit, by its <code>content-length</code> or as it
					arrived.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>Redirects</strong> are followed like a browser's <code>fetch</code>, 20 at most. A 303 (or a
				301 or 302 answering a POST) becomes a GET without its body; <code>authorization</code> and
				<code>cookie</code> headers aren't sent to another origin. <strong>TLS</strong> is rustls, checked
				against the web's certificate authorities. <strong>Nothing is decompressed:</strong> if you send
				<code>accept-encoding</code>, you get the body as the server sent it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="connections">Connections and proxies</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_partner.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_partner.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Connections are kept alive and reused by the next request to the same place (scheme, host, port
				and proxy), so an API you call often costs one TCP and TLS handshake, not one per request. An idle
				connection is kept 30 seconds, or a second less than its server says
				(<code>keep-alive: timeout=5</code>); at most 32 idle per place, any number open. If the server
				closed a kept connection meanwhile, the request is retried once on a new one, if it was never sent
				or is safe to send twice (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE, PUT, DELETE, or with an
				<code>idempotency-key</code> header), as Go's client does.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The environment's proxy</strong> (<code>FromEnv</code>, the default), read as Go reads it:
					<code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> for <code>https</code> URLs, <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> for
					<code>http</code>. Never for <code>localhost</code>, a loopback address, or a host
					<code>NO_PROXY</code> names. <code>NO_PROXY</code> takes <code>*</code>, an address, a range
					(<code>10.0.0.0/8</code>), <code>example.com</code> (that name and every name under it) or
					<code>.example.com</code> (only names under it), each with an optional <code>:port</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A request's own proxy</strong>: <code>Direct</code> uses none; <code>{"Via(url)"}</code>
					uses that proxy for every host. A proxy URL is <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>; a
					user and password in it go as <code>proxy-authorization</code>. An unreadable proxy URL is an
					<code>HttpErr</code>, never a silent way around the proxy.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Through the proxy</strong>, an <code>http</code> request goes to the proxy whole. An
					<code>https</code> one goes through a <code>CONNECT</code> tunnel with TLS to the server inside,
					so the proxy sees none of it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="chunks">Reading an answer as it arrives</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_lines.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_lines.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Http.fetch_chunks!(url, init, start, step!)"}</code> makes the same request as
				<code>fetch!</code> but never holds the whole answer. It calls <code>step!</code> with
				<code>{"Head(res)"}</code> first (status and headers, empty body), then <code>{"Chunk(bytes)"}</code>
				for each part of the body as it arrives. Like a fold, each call gets the previous return value,
				starting from <code>start</code>; the value after the last chunk is the result. If
				<code>step!</code> returns an <code>Err</code>, the request stops and its connection closes
				(<code>{"Err(Stopped(why))"}</code>).
			</p>
			<p>
				Use it for a body too large to hold, or one to pass on as it comes, like a language model's answer
				sent word by word to a <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#streams"}>stream of events</a>.
				<code>timeout_ms</code> runs to the last byte and <code>max_bytes</code> counts every chunk, so set
				both to 0 for a long stream.
			</p>

			<Heading id="bytes">Request bodies are bytes</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_digest.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_digest.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A request's <code>body</code> is a <code>List(U8)</code>, never decoded on the way: an upload is
				exactly what the client sent. <code>Server.text_of(request)</code> reads it as UTF-8 (U+FFFD where
				invalid). <code>{"Server.bytes(status, headers, bytes)"}</code> answers with raw bytes.
			</p>
			<p>
				An <a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoint</a> decodes its input from the body only when it
				is JSON (<code>application/json</code>, or a type ending in <code>+json</code>) or a form
				(URL-encoded, or <code>multipart/form-data</code>, whose files come as their names). For any other
				type (an image, a video), give the handler the input type <code>{"{}"}</code> and read
				<code>request.body</code> yourself; a handler that expects fields answers such a body 415. A
				multipart form's files are <code>Server.files(request)</code>, or one by field with
				<code>Server.file(request, field)</code>, each with its name, type and bytes.
			</p>
			<p>
				A route file may name the rules its body is checked with (<code>post_body = Signup.form</code>, from
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_forms}>packages/forms</a>). Form fields and a JSON object are then read
				alike, and what the rules refuse never reaches the handler: it gets 422, with the issues as JSON
				(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoints</a>).
			</p>
			<p>
				A request's body is read whole before your code runs, 16 MiB at most unless the server's
				<code>--max-body</code> (<code>MAX_BODY</code>) allows more
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running it</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="forward">Passing on a body from elsewhere</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_cdn.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_cdn.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Server.forward(upstream, headers)"}</code> is an answer whose body never passes through
				Roc. The host asks <code>upstream</code> and streams the answer on, so a 500 MB video costs your
				code nothing and starts playing at once. The answer takes upstream's status (200, 206 for a range,
				304, 404) and its body's headers (<code>content-type</code>, <code>content-length</code>,
				<code>content-range</code>, <code>accept-ranges</code>, <code>etag</code>,
				<code>last-modified</code>); headers you give are added or replace those.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Only what your code says goes upstream.</strong> The host adds nothing from the request,
					so its cookies stay here. <code>Server.passed_on(request)</code> gives the request's
					<code>Range</code>, <code>If-Range</code>, <code>If-None-Match</code> and
					<code>If-Modified-Since</code> headers to pass on. Change the request record first (drop a range
					that doesn't fit, say) to change what is asked.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An error upstream</strong> (400 or more) is answered with its status and standard reason
					as text, never upstream's body, which might name a bucket. No answer is a 502 (a 504 after 30 s),
					logged without the URL's query, where a presigned URL keeps its signature. A body cut short
					closes the client's connection, so it never passes as complete.
				</li>
				<li>
					The storage package's <code>Storage.serve!</code> is a forward to S3 for an S3 bucket
					(<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_storage}#serve"}>storage</a>); for a directory or memory, the server
					sends the file it keeps (<code>Server.stored</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="cookies">Cookies</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_cookies.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_cookies.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Server.set_cookie(answer, name, value, options)"}</code> adds a <code>Set-Cookie</code>
				header. <code>Server.CookieOpts</code> holds its attributes. <code>Server.cookie_defaults</code>,
				changed with a record update, are: path <code>/</code>, <code>http_only</code>,
				<code>same_site: Lax</code>, this host only, until the browser closes. When the app's public URL
				is HTTPS, the server marks every cookie it sets <code>Secure</code>, so leaving
				<code>secure</code> off only matters over plain HTTP in development.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>max_age_s</code> (<code>Max-Age</code>) and <code>expires_ms</code> (<code>Expires</code>, in
					milliseconds since the epoch) are left out when 0. With neither, the cookie ends when the browser
					closes.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>secure</code>, <code>http_only</code>, <code>same_site</code> (<code>Lax</code>,
					<code>Strict</code> or <code>None</code>), <code>path</code> and <code>domain</code> are each left
					out when empty. Browsers drop a <code>__Host-</code> cookie unless it is <code>secure</code> with
					path <code>/</code> and no domain, and drop <code>SameSite=None</code> without <code>secure</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Server.clear_cookie</code> takes the path, domain and <code>secure</code> the cookie was set
					with. <code>Server.with_cookie</code> and <code>Server.without_cookie</code> set and clear one with
					the defaults. The value is <code>%XX</code>-encoded; <code>Server.cookie(request, name)</code>
					decodes it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="routes">.well-known, and every method</Heading>
			<Code
				title="routes/"
				source={".well-known/oauth-authorization-server/+server.roc   # GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server\nuploads/[name]/+server.roc                             # put!: an upload, its body the bytes\nmcp/+server.roc                                        # respond!: every method, as it likes"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				A <code>.well-known</code> directory is routed like any other, so what other servers look for there
				(an OAuth server's metadata, <code>security.txt</code>) is an ordinary route file. Other directories
				starting with a dot aren't routed.
			</p>
			<p>
				A <code>+server.roc</code> usually defines one function per method (<code>get!</code>,
				<code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>…). <code>respond!</code> answers every method the file doesn't
				name: given the request, the path's params and the context, it returns its own
				<code>Server.Response</code>. Use it for a protocol that speaks POST and GET at one URL, or a page a
				package draws (<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_jobs}#page"}>the jobs page</a>). It isn't an endpoint, so
				it stays out of <code>/openapi.json</code> (<a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoints</a>).
			</p>
			<Code source={"## Every method, as it comes.\nrespond! : { request : Server.Request, params : {}, context : c } => Server.Response\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Server.response(200, [{ name: \"content-type\", value: \"text/plain\" }], \"\${request.method} \${Server.text_of(request)}\")"} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>HTTP/1.1 only</strong>, each way: no HTTP/2, and no SOCKS proxy.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A request's body is whole</strong> in Roc, in or out: no streaming. (Another server's
					answer can be read in chunks, and a forwarded body never enters Roc.)
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
